    "title": "Bob Rock at The Plant — Same Studios, Second Chapter",
    "content": "Reload was recorded in the same facilities as Load — The Plant Studios in Sausalito and Prairie Sun Recording in Cotati — and Bob Rock applied the same production philosophy to both records. The natural-room, lower-reverb approach that characterised the Load drum sound carried directly into Reload: moderate room microphone placement, reduced gated reverb on the snare, and a production aesthetic that allowed the Tama Star Classic Maple's natural voice to define the drum sound rather than processing it into the cavernous ambience of the Black Album.\n\n**The Plant Studios, Sausalito:**\nThe Plant's large tracking room had been the primary recording environment for Load, and its acoustic character — spacious but controlled, suited to natural room ambience — remained central to the Reload sessions. By the time Reload's tracks were recorded, Bob Rock's approach to the room had been refined through more than a year of Load sessions: he knew where to place the room mics for natural tone, how the Star Classic Maple responded in the space, and how to capture the kit's full acoustic character without reverb engineering.\n\n**Prairie Sun Recording, Cotati CA:**\nPrairie Sun's residential-style studio environment continued to serve the Reload sessions, offering tonal variety and a different acoustic atmosphere from The Plant. The sessions moved between both facilities across the extended recording process, contributing to the breadth of sonic environments audible across the eleven tracks.\n\n**Consistent Recording Philosophy:**\nThe core recording approach from Load carried through unchanged: reduced gated reverb on the snare, room microphones at moderate distances capturing natural tone rather than theatrical reverb, and a production strategy that trusted the Star Classic's natural resonance to carry the drum sound. What changed between Load and Reload was not the technical approach but the material: heavier, more varied tracks demanded different performance emphases from Lars, and the production adapted accordingly.\n\n**Microphone Setup:**\n- Kick drum: Shure Beta 52A inside, AKG D112 outside — same as Load\n- Snare: Shure SM57 top, Sennheiser MD421 bottom\n- Toms: Sennheiser MD421 on each tom\n- Hi-hat: AKG C451 small-diaphragm condenser\n- Overheads: Neumann U87s — tighter placement than Black Album\n- Room mics: Moderate placement, natural room tone consistent with Load approach",
